%S A399210 1,1,0,0,1,0,2,4,16,54,222,891,3749,16010,69840,308825,1384129,
%T A399210 6272298,28713202,132622689,617576213,2897102043,13682539896,
%U A399210 65021182292,310754495450,1493022902939,7208349564169,34960436386943,170277130768992,832634346588458,4086603234992982
%N A399210 Number of distinct simple Baxter permutations of length n up to action of the dihedral group of the square D_4 on the permutation matrix.
%C A399210 Simple is meant in the sense of A111111: the only intervals mapped onto intervals are the singletons and the whole permutation. As in A397853, the permutations 1, 12 and 21 are regarded as simple.
%C A399210 The Baxter class is closed under the full dihedral action on permutation matrices, and simplicity is invariant under this action. Thus the restriction to simple Baxter permutations is a union of D_4-orbits.
%C A399210 Equivalently, a(n) is the number of prime mosaic floorplans with n rooms, up to mosaic equivalence and the rotations and reflections of the enclosing rectangle. Here prime means that no set of at least 2 and at most n-1 rooms has rectangular union. The Ackerman-Barequet-Pinter bijection identifies blocks of a mosaic floorplan with intervals of its Baxter permutation.
%C A399210 This differs from A340984: that sequence uses generic rectangulation equivalence and also includes 4-crossings (four rectangles meeting at a point), whereas the present sequence uses mosaic equivalence (equivalently, Baxter permutations).
%H A399210 Meghanto Majumder, <a href="/A399210/b399210.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..500</a>
%H A399210 E. Ackerman, G. Barequet and R. Y. Pinter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2006.03.018">A bijection between permutations and floorplans, and its applications</a>, Discrete Appl. Math. 154 (2006), 1674-1684.
%H A399210 M. H. Albert and M. D. Atkinson, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2005.06.016">Simple permutations and pattern restricted permutations</a>, Discrete Math. 300 (2005), 1-15.
%H A399210 R. Brignall, <a href="https://www.rbrignall.org.uk/papers/PhDThesis.pdf">Simplicity in Relational Structures and its Application to Permutation Classes</a>, PhD thesis, University of St Andrews, 2007. Section 1.2 notes that the eight symmetries preserve intervals and hence simplicity.
%H A399210 K. Dilks, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.07007">Quarter-Turn Baxter Permutations</a>, arXiv:1710.07007 [math.CO], 2017. Section 1 describes the D_4 action on Baxter permutations.
%H A399210 Meghanto Majumder, <a href="/A399210/a399210.txt">PARI/GP program to compute the sequence</a>.
%F A399210 a(n) = (s(n) + 2*q(n) + h(n) + 2*d(n) + 2*[n=1])/8, where s(n) = A397853(n), q(n) counts simple Baxter permutations fixed by a quarter-turn, h(n) counts those fixed by a half-turn, and d(n) counts simple involutive Baxter permutations. This is Burnside's lemma for the five symmetry types in D_4.
%e A399210 For n = 5, the simple Baxter permutations 25314 and 41352 form one D_4-orbit, so a(5) = 1.
%e A399210 For n = 7 there are two orbits. One has size 8 and representative 2475316; the other has size 4 and representative 2631574.
%Y A399210 Cf. A397853 (simple Baxter permutations), A398832 (all Baxter permutations up to D_4), A111111 (simple permutations), A001181 (Baxter permutations), A340984 (prime generic rectangulations up to equivalence).
